<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- Entitlements for the HOST only: the pinned Node runtime and the native
       code it loads. Do not apply these to the UI shell, which needs none of
       them. `scripts/sign-macos-app.sh` applies this file to the payloads and
       the `node` binary, and signs the application bundle separately.

         Derived from Node's tools/osx-entitlements.plist for the pinned runtime,
       then reduced to what the shipped application demonstrably requires.

       Node upstream grants five entitlements. Each of the other four was tested
       against the real bundle, signing every Mach-O payload it ships (the
       Keychain addon, sqlite-vec's dylib and Temporal's core-bridge) and
       exercising V8 startup, a Keychain round trip, a sqlite-vec extension load
       and a core-bridge load:

         allow-unsigned-executable-memory  NOT required
         disable-library-validation        NOT required once the shipped native
                                           code is signed with this identity;
                                           keeping it off preserves the
                                           protection ADR 0026 assumes
         allow-dyld-environment-variables  NOT required
         get-task-allow                    a debug entitlement permitting any
                                           process to attach a debugger;
                                           notarization rejects it

       Only JIT remains. `scripts/sign-macos-app.sh` performs the signing this
       depends on: native payloads first, then the bundle.
  -->

  <!-- V8 compiles JavaScript at runtime. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
